## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design in-place sorting algorithms, understand stability, and reason about trade-offs between time and memory in constrained environments.

## Key Concepts

- In-place merging
- Stability
- Time/space complexity
- Rotation-based merging

## Code Example

```javascript
function inPlaceMerge(arr, left, mid, right) {
  let i = left, j = mid + 1;
  while (i <= mid && j <= right) {
    if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) { i++; continue; }
    const tmp = arr[j];
    for (let k = j; k > i; k--) arr[k] = arr[k - 1];
    arr[i] = tmp;
    i++; mid++; j++;
  }
}

function inPlaceMergeSort(arr, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1) {
  if (left >= right) return;
  const mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);
  inPlaceMergeSort(arr, left, mid);
  inPlaceMergeSort(arr, mid + 1, right);
  inPlaceMerge(arr, left, mid, right);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize rotations to guarantee O(n log n) time in all cases?
- How does memory locality affect performance compared with standard mergesort?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to convert dependency graphs into scheduling via topological order; checks understanding of longest-path DP on DAG, cycle detection, and clear complexity analysis.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort (Kahn / DFS)
- DAG longest path DP
- Cycle detection
- Complexity analysis

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestFinishTimes(n, edges, durations) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  const finish = durations.slice();
  let seen = 0;
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    seen++;
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      finish[v] = Math.max(finish[v], finish[u] + durations[v]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  if (seen !== n) throw new Error('Cycle detected');
  return finish;
}
```

